VobTree: parse and skip the VOb tree iteratively, so its depth is not the call stack.

`parse_vob_tree` recursed once per child, and the `skip` lambda it uses for an
object it could not parse recursed once per nesting level too. Nothing bounds
either depth: it is the file's own `childs<N>` counts that say how deep the tree
goes, and a nested node is cheap to write -- an object header and a child count.

This is the same defect patch `0035` removed from `_parse_bsp_nodes`, and it is
cheaper to trigger. Measured against `zenkit-node`'s 1.4 KB fixture with one
chain of nested VObs spliced into the root's children, on node's 8 MB main
thread (the editor loads worlds on a `worker_threads` worker, whose default
stack is 4 MB -- half of that):

- 60,000 nested `zCVob`s (a 9 MB file) kill the process with 0xC00000FD, a
  stack overflow, which no `catch` can turn into a thrown error;
- 200,000 nested *empty* (`%`) objects (a 5 MB file) kill it the same way
  through the `skip` lambda, which is the cheaper of the two paths on the wire
  because nothing between the object header and the child count is read.

The fix is not a depth limit, for the reason `0035` gives: the depth a valid
world may reach rises with its size and ZenGin documents no ceiling, so a
guessed limit risks refusing a world the engine loads. Both walks keep their own
stack of the children still owed instead, so depth costs heap and not the call
stack.

The read order is unchanged and deliberately so -- the entry stream is read
positionally, so the order of the reads *is* the format. A node is read, then
its child count, then its whole subtree before the next sibling, and a child is
appended to its parent in the order it is read.

`read_vob_node` is the node read the two loops share: it returns the object and
its child count, and reports a count of zero for an object that is not a VOb,
since its children are skipped rather than attached. That is what the recursive
version did with the early `return nullptr` after `skip`.

Upstreamable as-is: no API change, no behaviour change on any valid world, and
the transformation is mechanical.
